    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 4\n    n_machines = 4\n    processing_times = [[12, 29, 30, 9], [28, 23, 25, 18], [10, 27, 10, 30], [30, 19, 21, 10]]\n    machine_assignments = [[1, 3, 0, 2], [0, 2, 3, 1], [2, 3, 0, 1], [3, 2, 1, 0]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Coordinated scheduling of current production batch across specialized workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized workstations that support discrete, routings-based production. The current production batch consists of multiple customer orders (jobs), each of which requires a defined series of operations performed in a fixed sequence. Every operation must run on a specific machine for a known processing duration.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nBecause jobs visit different machines at different points in their routes, the timing and order in which we allocate machines to operations will significantly affect total lead time. Uncoordinated dispatching can create idle time on some machines while other jobs queue, increasing overall completion time. We need a consolidated production schedule that coordinates all operations across the shop so that machines are well utilized and jobs flow without unnecessary waiting.\n\nKey Requirements and Rules\n- Machine exclusivity: Each workstation can process only one operation at a time. There is no parallel processing on a single machine.\n- Fixed job sequences: Operations within each job must be completed in the exact order defined by its routing. An operation cannot begin until its predecessor for that job has finished.\n- Machine- and time-specific tasks: Each operation is assigned to a designated machine and must run for its specified processing time on that machine.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it must run to completion without interruption.\n- Feasibility: The schedule must avoid overlaps on any single machine and respect the required job sequences.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a finite-capacity production schedule that sequences all operations across the available machines to complete all jobs in the shortest possible total time (minimize the makespan), while maintaining flow and avoiding unnecessary machine idle time.\n\nNext Steps\n- Consolidate the operation sequences, designated machines, and processing times for all jobs.\n- Build an initial feasible schedule that respects all ordering and capacity rules.\n- Iteratively refine the sequence on each machine to reduce waiting, smooth bottlenecks, and compress the overall completion time.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 4\n- Number of machines (workstations): 4\n- Machine assignments by job/operation: Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 3\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 3, Op3: Machine 0, Op4: Machine 2]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 3, Op4: Machine 1]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 3, Op3: Machine 0, Op4: Machine 1]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 3, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 1, Op4: Machine 0]\n- Processing times by job/operation: Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 4 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 12, Op2: 29, Op3: 30, Op4: 9]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 28, Op2: 23, Op3: 25, Op4: 18]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 10, Op2: 27, Op3: 10, Op4: 30]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 30, Op2: 19, Op3: 21, Op4: 10]",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Coordinated scheduling of current production batch across specialized workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized workstations that support discrete, routings-based production. The current production batch consists of multiple customer orders (jobs), each of which requires a defined series of operations performed in a fixed sequence. Every operation must run on a specific machine for a known processing duration.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nBecause jobs visit different machines at different points in their routes, the timing and order in which we allocate machines to operations will significantly affect total lead time. Uncoordinated dispatching can create idle time on some machines while other jobs queue, increasing overall completion time. We need a consolidated production schedule that coordinates all operations across the shop so that machines are well utilized and jobs flow without unnecessary waiting.\n\nKey Requirements and Rules\n- Machine exclusivity: Each workstation can process only one operation at a time. There is no parallel processing on a single machine.\n- Fixed job sequences: Operations within each job must be completed in the exact order defined by its routing. An operation cannot begin until its predecessor for that job has finished.\n- Machine- and time-specific tasks: Each operation is assigned to a designated machine and must run for its specified processing time on that machine.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it must run to completion without interruption.\n- Feasibility: The schedule must avoid overlaps on any single machine and respect the required job sequences.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a finite-capacity production schedule that sequences all operations across the available machines to complete all jobs in the shortest possible total time (minimize the makespan), while maintaining flow and avoiding unnecessary machine idle time.\n\nNext Steps\n- Consolidate the operation sequences, designated machines, and processing times for all jobs.\n- Build an initial feasible schedule that respects all ordering and capacity rules.\n- Iteratively refine the sequence on each machine to reduce waiting, smooth bottlenecks, and compress the overall completion time.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Machine assignments by job/operation: {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}\n- Processing times by job/operation: {PROCESSING_TIMES}"
